# An Interactive Journey Through Transfinite Survival

Begin with a finite challenge: whatever horizon is proposed, choose one more round. {{interactive_demo:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the first cofinality argument</summary>The finite durations are precisely the natural-number ordinals. Every one is below $\omega$, but for every finite cap $N$, the successor $N+1$ exceeds it. Therefore their supremum is $\omega$ and is not attained.</details>

## From rounds to blocks

The second counter counts finite tails inside successive $\omega$-blocks. Explore the exact symbolic clock. {{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal why the exact supremum is $\omega^2$</summary>For fixed $k$, finite tails have supremum $\omega k+\omega=\omega(k+1)$. Every reading lies below $\omega^2$, while the subfamily $\omega k$ is cofinal in $\omega^2$. The upper and lower bounds coincide.</details>

## Local versus global bounds

Each play has a finite budget, but no one finite budget controls all plays. {{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

## Birthdays of dyadic games

Arithmetic size and construction age move in opposite directions. {{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the birthday calculation</summary>The canonical dyadic unit $2^{-n}$ has birthday $n+1$. Hence birthdays are cofinal in $\omega$, and the weighted ranks $\omega(n+1)$ are cofinal in $\omega^2$.</details>

## Continue exploring

Background: [ordinal numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordinal_number), [ordinal arithmetic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordinal_arithmetic), [surreal numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surreal_number), and [infinite-time Turing machines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinite-time_Turing_machine). {{demo:1}}

